<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- The running configuration for the QEMU q35 two-dataplane-port slice.

     This document is the single source of the appliance's addressing. Three
     things read it and none of them restates it: `pds/config` embeds it and
     commits it at boot; `tools/xtask/src/topology.rs` reads the bench out of
     it, which is where `qemu.rs` takes the MAC it gives each guest NIC and
     where `forward_harness.rs` takes the endpoints the routed contract is
     stated between; and `tools/xtask/src/config_transcript.rs` derives from it
     the console records a boot must produce. Those used to be three literals
     that had to agree and that nothing compared.

     A guest NIC therefore cannot be given a MAC no interface here claims, and
     the contract cannot expect a station this file has not attached to a port.

     Hardware topology is not configurable here: which ports
     exist, and the protection domains and memory regions behind them, are fixed
     in librefirewall.system at build time. What a port *is* — its addressing
     and whether it is used at all — is this file.

     The `<management>` element is the appliance's own presence on the dedicated
     management port, which carries no forwarded traffic, and it is a sibling of `<interfaces>`
     rather than an entry inside it because that port is not a dataplane port: it
     has no `port` number, it is not in the router's port set, and `config`'s
     PORT_COUNT is 2 whatever this file says. What reads it is the management
     protection domain, which maps the configuration region READ-ONLY and answers
     ARP and ICMP echo for the address below and for nothing else; the dataplane
     forwards nothing to it and cannot reach it.

     The `<rules>` element is the filter policy, and it is the one element whose
     order means something: the appliance matches first-match-wins and drops
     what nothing matched, so a rule's line number is its precedence and an
     empty `<rules/>` forwards nothing. Every criterion is written out, `any`
     included — a wildcard an operator can see is the point of a filter
     document, and there is no attribute here whose omission widens a rule.

     The two rules are what the forwarding contract is stated against.
     `probe-forward` is why the routed probes are routed at all; `probe-blocked`
     names a destination port nothing else uses, so a packet the appliance can
     route is dropped by policy rather than by any routing reason; and a packet
     matching neither falls past the last rule to the default deny. The three
     outcomes are distinguishable by drop reason and by per-rule counter, which
     is what `tools/xtask/src/forward_harness.rs` asserts.

     THESE RULES ARE MOSTLY ABOUT WHICH FLOWS MAY OPEN. A packet an established
     flow already accounts for is forwarded before the filter is consulted at all,
     so `probe-forward` decides the *first* packet of a conversation and the rest
     of it travels on the flow that packet opened. That is why there is no rule
     here about established traffic and no criterion to write one with: a reply
     comes back because the appliance recognises the conversation, not because a
     line of this document permits it.

     The `tracking` criterion is where the other thing that reaches the filter is
     named: traffic an existing conversation is the *reason* for without belonging
     to it, which today means an ICMP error quoting one of its datagrams. Such an
     error is composed by whoever sent it, with a source address of its choosing,
     so it is decided by these rules like anything else — and this document names
     no rule admitting it, so it is denied. `probe-forward` says `opening` rather
     than `any` for exactly that reason: an `accept` written for a conversation's
     first packet must not quietly also admit an error somebody else composed
     about it.

     What it costs is stated where it is paid: a packet the connection tracker
     cannot keep state for — a fragment, a protocol it does not decode, a TCP
     segment from the middle of a conversation it never saw begin — is refused
     before the filter, so no rule here can permit one. And editing these rules
     changes which conversations may *start*, so on the packet path an edit does
     not reach one already running — what reaches one is the commit itself, which
     re-decides the whole flow table and takes back every conversation the new
     rules no longer admit.

     Its address is 10.0.2.15/24, which is not an arbitrary choice: QEMU's
     user-mode (SLIRP) stack places the guest at 10.0.2.15 with the gateway at
     10.0.2.2, so `make run` reaches this port with no extra configuration, and
     the /24 it sits in is disjoint from both dataplane prefixes above — which
     `config`'s validation refuses to let it stop being.

     Its `gateway` is that same 10.0.2.2, and it is the one attribute here that
     no `<interface>` carries. A gateway is read by the outbound dial of the
     port that holds it, and this is the only port that dials: the dataplane
     originates no traffic of its own, so a gateway beside an interface above
     would be a value nothing in this build reads. A port that reaches only its
     own link writes `gateway="none"` rather than leaving the attribute out,
     because an omission and a misspelling look the same to an operator. -->
<configuration>
    <interfaces>
        <interface id="dataplane-0" port="0" enabled="true"
                   mac="52:54:00:12:34:50" address="10.0.0.1" prefix-length="24"/>
        <interface id="dataplane-1" port="1" enabled="true"
                   mac="52:54:00:12:34:51" address="10.0.1.1" prefix-length="24"/>
    </interfaces>
    <neighbours>
        <neighbour id="endpoint-a" interface="dataplane-0"
                   address="10.0.0.2" mac="52:54:00:00:00:0a"/>
        <neighbour id="endpoint-b" interface="dataplane-1"
                   address="10.0.1.2" mac="52:54:00:00:00:0b"/>
    </neighbours>
    <rules>
        <rule id="probe-blocked" ingress="any" egress="any"
              source="any" destination="any" protocol="udp"
              source-port="any" destination-port="5001" icmp-type="any" tracking="any" action="drop"/>
        <rule id="probe-forward" ingress="any" egress="any"
              source="any" destination="any" protocol="udp"
              source-port="any" destination-port="5000" icmp-type="any" tracking="opening" action="accept"/>
    </rules>
    <management mac="52:54:00:12:34:52" address="10.0.2.15"
                prefix-length="24" enabled="true" gateway="10.0.2.2"/>
</configuration>
